;
; DEVS:NetInterfaces/eth0 -- IPv6 only, no IPv4 at all
;
; Use this shape on a network that runs IPv6 and where this machine has no
; business holding an IPv4 address: a lab segment, a machine reached only
; over IPv6, or a network whose IPv4 addresses have run out.
;
; Copy this into DEVS:NetInterfaces as "eth0", then either reboot or type
;
;     C:AddNetInterface DEVS:NetInterfaces/eth0
;
; Two keywords carry the whole of it.  CONFIGURE=NONE switches IPv4 off, so
; nothing waits for an address that is never coming and nothing falls back to
; a 169.254 one.  CONFIGURE6 says where the IPv6 address comes from.
;
; A build without IPv6 cannot use this file: it recognises CONFIGURE6 and
; ignores it, so the interface would be left with no address of either
; family and AddNetInterface will say so.
;

DEVICE=a2065.device
UNIT=0

; No IPv4 on this interface.
CONFIGURE=NONE

; Where IPv6 comes from.
;
;   AUTO    follow the router.  Its advertisement carries a prefix to build
;           an address from, and two flags: "managed" means ask a DHCPv6
;           server for an address, "other" means ask one for the name
;           servers.  AUTO does whatever the router asks for, which is what
;           makes this file work on a network nobody has described to it.
;   DHCP    ask a DHCPv6 server for an address, without waiting to be told
;           to.  For a network that runs one and whose router does not say
;           so.
;   STATIC  use the ADDRESS6 below.
;
CONFIGURE6=AUTO

; With CONFIGURE6=STATIC these two are what the interface uses.  The prefix
; length goes on the address; 64 is the usual one and is the default.
;
;ADDRESS6=2001:db8::10/64
;GATEWAY6=fe80::1

; The name servers arrive with the address: in a router advertisement (RFC
; 8106), or in the DHCPv6 reply.  Where both answer, DHCPv6 is asked first,
; because the router's own advertisement is what sent this machine to it.
; DEVS:Internet/name_resolution can hold a NAMESERVER line as well, and what
; is written there wins over both.
